const LESSON=[{"number": 1, "key": "slide-1", "title": "Is It Any Good? Looking at, measuring, showing and signing AI art", "script": "Good evening, and welcome to Week 10. For nine weeks I have taught you how to make things: images, motion, long takes, voices, worlds. Tonight is a different kind of class. Tonight we ask the question every artist hears at the end. Is it any good? And how would you know? There is more art than usual tonight, about two thirds, and about one third technique. By the end you will be able to run a proper critique, read a leaderboard without being fooled, and sign your own work. You need all three for your final presentation in two weeks.", "zh": "欢迎来到第十周。前九周我们学怎么做：图像、运动、长镜头、声音、世界。今晚换个问题：它到底好不好？你怎么知道？今晚大约三分之二讲艺术，三分之一讲技术。结束时你要会做一次真正的点评，会看排行榜而不被骗，会给自己的作品署名。两周后的期末展示三样都用得上。", "section": "Opening", "source": ""}, {"number": 2, "key": "slide-2", "title": "Rate this picture from 1 to 10. Hold up your fingers.", "script": "Let's start with a game. Look at this picture for ten seconds. A grand hall, figures in robes, a big round window of light. Now give it a score from one to ten and hold up your fingers. Don't look at your neighbour. Good. Remember your number, because I am going to ask you for it again in one minute.", "zh": "先玩个游戏。看这张图十秒：宏大的厅堂、穿长袍的人、巨大的圆形光窗。给它打1到10分，举手指，不要看旁边的人。记住你的分数，一分钟后我再问一次。", "section": "Opening", "source": ""}, {"number": 3, "key": "slide-3", "title": "Now you know: it won a prize, then it was refused copyright", "script": "Here is what you just looked at. It is called Théâtre D'opéra Spatial, by Jason Allen. In 2022 it won first prize in the digital art category at the Colorado State Fair. He made it with Midjourney, and he says he worked through at least 624 prompts and then edited it by hand. He credited Midjourney on the entry, but the two judges did not know it was an AI. When they found out, they said they would have given him the prize anyway. The public was furious. The US Copyright Office later refused to register it, and that case is still in court today. Now, hold up your number again. Did it go up? Did it go down? The picture has not changed by one pixel. Only what you know about it has changed. Remember that. It is the whole lecture in one minute.", "zh": "这张图叫《太空歌剧院》，作者 Jason Allen。2022年它在科罗拉多州博览会拿了数字艺术一等奖，用 Midjourney 做，他说至少用了624条提示词，再手工修改。评委当时不知道它是AI，事后说照样会给奖；公众却很愤怒。美国版权局后来拒绝登记，官司至今未结。现在再举一次手：分数变了吗？图一个像素都没变，变的只是你知道的信息。这就是今晚的全部内容。", "section": "Opening", "source": "Sources: US Copyright Office review board letter (Sept 2023); New York Times, 2 Sept 2022; Mayer Brown, “Supreme Court denies review in AI authorship case”, 11 March 2026."}, {"number": 4, "key": "slide-4", "title": "Four stories from this year", "script": "We have four stories tonight, the same format as the last three weeks. One: looking. How do you actually critique a work made with AI? Two: measuring. This is the technical part. What do the numbers in the papers mean, and what can they never see? Three: showing. What happens when the work leaves your laptop and goes into a museum or a festival? Four: signing. What do you owe your audience, and how does the technology of labels really work? Almost everything tonight happened in the last few months, some of it in the last few weeks.", "zh": "今晚四个故事。一，看：怎么点评 AI 作品。二，量：技术部分，论文里的数字是什么意思，它们看不到什么。三，展：作品走进美术馆和电影节之后发生什么。四，署名：你欠观众什么，标注技术到底怎么工作。几乎都是最近几个月甚至几周的事。", "section": "Opening", "source": ""}, {"number": 5, "key": "slide-5", "title": "Where we’ve been: you have already asked this question", "script": "You have been circling this question all term. In Lecture 4 I said a technical score answers only a narrow question. In Lecture 5 we asked whose model it is. In Lecture 7 Sora disappeared, and we asked what a work needs to outlive its tool. In Lecture 8 we saw a model beat real video on a lip-sync score, and I promised we would come back to that trap. Last week we asked who owns a scan of a place. Tonight we pull these threads together.", "zh": "这个问题我们绕了一学期。第4讲：技术分数只回答很窄的问题。第5讲：模型是谁的。第7讲：Sora消失了，作品怎么比工具活得长。第8讲：模型的唇同步分数超过真视频，我说过会回来讲这个陷阱。上周：扫描的副本归谁。今晚把这些线收起来。", "section": "Opening", "source": ""}, {"number": 6, "key": "slide-6", "title": "How top courses teach this: two different classrooms", "script": "I looked at how other universities teach this, and there are really two classrooms. In the engineering classroom, at Stanford and CMU, evaluation means a number. I will use Jun-Yan Zhu's slides from CMU and Stefano Ermon's from Stanford. In the art classroom, at Harvard, and in courses like Eryk Salvaggio's and Hamilton's How to Read AI Images, evaluation means slow looking and arguing. Almost nobody teaches both in one night. That is what we will try. Engineering asks how close is it. Art asks what does it do to you. You need both answers.", "zh": "我看了其他大学怎么教，其实有两种教室。工程教室（斯坦福、CMU）里，评价是一个数字，我会用朱俊彦和 Stefano Ermon 的幻灯片。艺术教室（哈佛、Salvaggio、Hamilton 学院的《如何阅读AI图像》）里，评价是慢慢看和争论。很少有人一晚讲两边，今晚试试。工程问多接近，艺术问它对你做了什么。两个答案都要。", "section": "Opening", "source": ""}, {"number": 7, "key": "slide-7", "title": "Looking", "script": "Story one: looking. Before any metric, before any law, somebody has to look at the thing. And it turns out that looking is a skill, it can be taught, and a generator has made it more valuable, because the one thing a generator cannot do for you is spend time.", "zh": "第一个故事：看。在任何指标和法律之前，总得有人看。看是一种技能，可以教。生成模型让它更值钱，因为模型唯一不能替你做的就是花时间。", "section": "Story 1 · Looking", "source": ""}, {"number": 8, "key": "slide-8", "title": "Every new medium gets asked: is this even art?", "script": "This question is old. In 1859 the poet Baudelaire called photography the refuge of every failed painter. In 1917 Duchamp signed a urinal, the exhibition refused to show it, and his friends wrote: he chose it. Around 1969 Vera Molnár was drawing with a plotter, and the art world mostly ignored her. In 2018 a GAN portrait sold at Christie's for 432,500 dollars. In 2022, the state fair. This year a museum of AI art opened in Los Angeles, and a month ago the Pope weighed in. Every time, the argument sounds new. Every time, people are also arguing about who gets to be called an artist.", "zh": "这个问题很老。1859年波德莱尔说摄影是失败画家的避难所。1917年杜尚签了一个小便池，展览拒展，朋友写道：他选择了它。1969年前后莫尔纳用绘图仪画画，艺术界几乎无视。2018年一幅GAN肖像在佳士得卖了43.25万美元。2022年是州博览会。今年洛杉矶开了AI艺术博物馆，一个月前教皇也发言了。每次争论都像新的，每次也都在争谁配叫艺术家。", "section": "Story 1 · Looking", "source": "Works shown for teaching and criticism. Baudelaire, Salon of 1859; The Blind Man no. 2 (1917); Christie’s, Oct 2018; Vatican, 2 Oct 2026."}, {"number": 9, "key": "slide-9", "title": "1859: Baudelaire hated photography. Then he sat for Nadar.", "script": "Here is Baudelaire in 1859. Photography, he says, is the refuge of every would-be painter, too lazy to finish his studies. It should go back to being the servant of the arts. You could post that on social media tonight and just change one word. And look at this cartoon from 1862. Daumier draws the photographer Nadar in a balloon, taking pictures over Paris, and every building below has a sign saying photography. The joke is: elevating photography to the height of art. It's a very good joke. And Baudelaire himself sat for portraits by Nadar. Those photographs are how we know his face today.", "zh": "1859年波德莱尔说：摄影是那些懒得完成学业的画家的避难所，应该回去做艺术的仆人。今晚发到社交媒体，只改一个词就行。1862年杜米埃画了纳达尔在气球上拍巴黎，下面每栋楼都挂着摄影招牌，标题是“把摄影提升到艺术的高度”。而波德莱尔自己也让纳达尔拍过肖像，我们今天就是靠这些照片认识他的脸。", "section": "Story 1 · Looking", "source": "Baudelaire, “The Modern Public and Photography”, Salon of 1859 (trans. J. Mayne). Daumier, lithograph, Le Boulevard, 1862. Public domain."}, {"number": 10, "key": "slide-10", "title": "1917: the first prompt engineer? “He CHOSE it.”", "script": "Now 1917. A factory urinal, signed R. Mutt, submitted to an open show in New York that had promised to accept everything. They refused it. And Duchamp's friends published this sentence: whether Mr Mutt with his own hands made the fountain or not has no importance. He chose it. That is still the best argument anyone has made for AI art. You did not paint it, you chose it. But be careful. Duchamp's choice cost him something. It was one act, against everybody, and it changed the question. If you choose the nicest of four outputs, a thousand times a day, with no risk at all, is that the same kind of choosing? That's for you to answer.", "zh": "1917年，一个工厂小便池，签名 R. Mutt，送去一个声称什么都收的展览，被拒。杜尚的朋友写道：他是否亲手做了这个喷泉并不重要，他选择了它。这至今是对AI艺术最好的辩护。但要小心：杜尚的选择是有代价的，一次，对抗所有人，改变了问题。你每天从四张输出里挑最好看的，毫无风险，那是同一种选择吗？", "section": "Story 1 · Looking", "source": "Alfred Stieglitz, photograph of Duchamp’s Fountain, 1917. Public domain. The Blind Man no. 2, May 1917."}, {"number": 11, "key": "slide-11", "title": "A month ago: the Pope and an artist, in public", "script": "This is very recent. On the second of October, Pope Leo spoke to artists and curators at the Vatican. He said there is an ontological difference, even before an aesthetic one, between art and what a machine can generate. And he said algorithms lack the spark of the human. Three weeks later, last Tuesday, an artist named Sarp Kerem Yavuz answered him with an open letter in The Art Newspaper, citing Duchamp, Cage and Warhol. Language itself, he says, has become a paintbrush. Look closely at the Pope's words: even before an aesthetic one. He is saying the question of quality comes second. If he's right, a beautiful AI film is still not art. So we need to separate these questions, which brings me to the most useful slide tonight.", "zh": "这很新。10月2日教皇对艺术家和策展人说：在审美之前，艺术和机器能生成的东西之间就有本体论的差别；算法缺少人的火花。上周二，艺术家 Yavuz 在《艺术新闻》发公开信回应，引用杜尚、凯奇、沃霍尔：语言本身已成为画笔。注意教皇说的是“在审美之前”，质量问题排第二。如果他对，再美的AI电影也不是艺术。所以我们要把问题分开。", "section": "Story 1 · Looking", "source": "Vatican.va, address of 2 October 2026; Hyperallergic, 5 October 2026; The Art Newspaper, 27 October 2026."}, {"number": 12, "key": "slide-12", "title": "Slop: the word of 2025, and what its opposite is", "script": "Merriam-Webster chose slop as the word of 2025. Digital content of low quality, produced usually in quantity, by means of AI. Two parts: low quality, and in quantity. A painter can make a bad painting, but only a machine can make bad work at this speed. So what is the opposite of slop? Students often say handmade. I don't think so. I've seen plenty of handmade rubbish. The opposite of slop is decided. Somebody chose this, and they can tell you why. Here is my test. Ask the maker: why this one, and not the other one? Slop has no answer to that question.", "zh": "韦氏词典选 slop 为2025年度词：通常由AI大量生产的低质量数字内容。两部分：低质量、大量。画家也能画烂画，但只有机器能这么快地产出烂东西。Slop 的反面是什么？不是“手工”，手工垃圾我见多了。反面是“被决定过的”：有人选了它，并能说出为什么。测试：问作者，为什么是这个不是那个？Slop 答不出来。", "section": "Story 1 · Looking", "source": "Merriam-Webster, 2025 Word of the Year."}, {"number": 13, "key": "slide-13", "title": "The most useful slide tonight: four questions people mix up", "script": "Here it is. There are four questions that people constantly mix up. One: is it AI? That is a question of fact, and you answer it with evidence. Two: is it good? That is judgment, you answer it by looking and arguing. Three: is it fair? That's ethics. Whose data, whose consent, whose job. Four: is it allowed? That is law, and it differs by country and even by festival. Most fights online happen because someone asks question two and someone else answers question three. A work can be beautiful and unfair. It can be fair and boring. When you critique, always say which question you are answering. Story 1 is mainly question two. Story 2 tries to turn it into a number. Stories 3 and 4 take the others.", "zh": "这页最有用。四个问题常被混淆。一，是AI吗？事实问题，靠证据。二，好吗？判断问题，靠看和争论。三，公平吗？伦理问题：谁的数据、谁同意、谁的工作。四，允许吗？法律问题，各国、各电影节都不同。网上吵架多半是有人问第二个，有人答第三个。作品可以美而不公平，也可以公平而无聊。点评时说清楚你在回答哪一个。", "section": "Story 1 · Looking", "source": ""}, {"number": 14, "key": "slide-14", "title": "The label changes the score. We have measured it.", "script": "You just did this with your fingers, and psychologists did it properly. In 2023 Lucas Bellaiche and colleagues took thirty paintings, all of them made by AI, and randomly told people some were by humans. Same images. People liked the human-labelled ones slightly more. They found them much more profound, and much more valuable. Look at the chart: the effect on liking is small, and the effect on worth is more than three times larger. So people's eyes barely change. What changes is the story they tell about value. There is one hopeful finding: when people felt a stronger story in the AI-labelled work, the gap shrank. It's correlational, but if you want a takeaway for your own work, it is this one. Give us a story.", "zh": "你刚才用手指做过了，心理学家做得更严谨。2023年 Bellaiche 等人用30幅全是AI画的画，随机告诉观众有些是人画的。同样的图，人们稍微更喜欢“人画的”，但觉得它深刻得多、值钱得多。对“喜欢”的影响很小，对“价值”大三倍多。眼睛几乎没变，变的是关于价值的故事。一个好消息：觉得AI作品有更强的故事时，差距缩小（相关性）。给观众一个故事。", "section": "Story 1 · Looking", "source": "Bellaiche et al., “Humans versus AI: whether and why we prefer human-created compared to AI-created artwork”, Cognitive Research 8:42 (2023). Study 1, n = 149."}, {"number": 15, "key": "slide-15", "title": "Harvard: three hours with one painting. What she saw, and when.", "script": "At Harvard the art historian Jennifer Roberts gives her students an assignment that sounds crazy. Pick one work, go to the museum, and look at it for three full hours. Students complain. Then they come back astonished. She did it herself with this painting by Copley, from 1765. At nine minutes she noticed that the boy's ear has exactly the same shape as the ruff on the squirrel's belly. At twenty-one minutes, that his fingers span exactly the width of the glass below. At forty-five minutes, that the folds of the curtain repeat the shapes of his ear and eye. None of this is visible in the first ten seconds. A person put every one of these there, on purpose.", "zh": "哈佛的 Jennifer Roberts 让学生选一件作品，在美术馆看满三小时。学生抱怨，回来后都震惊了。她自己看科普利1765年这幅画：第9分钟发现男孩的耳朵和松鼠肚子上的毛形状一样；第21分钟发现手指正好跨过下面玻璃杯的直径；第45分钟发现窗帘褶皱重复耳朵和眼睛的形状。前十秒都看不到，而这些都是人有意放进去的。", "section": "Story 1 · Looking", "source": "Jennifer L. Roberts, “The Power of Patience”, Harvard Magazine, Nov–Dec 2013. Copley, 1765, Museum of Fine Arts, Boston. Public domain."}, {"number": 16, "key": "slide-16", "title": "A generated image has no nine-minute detail, unless you put one there.", "script": "So here's the uncomfortable thought. In a painting like that, the time of making leaves traces, and looking slowly gets you closer to those decisions. A generated image also has details, millions of them. But nobody decided them. If you look at one for nine minutes, you often find nothing, or you find the training data, which we will come to. That's not a reason to give up. It's a task. Look at your own clip for three minutes. Ask what is in there on purpose. If the honest answer is nothing, that's your next edit.", "zh": "不太舒服的想法：油画里，制作的时间留下痕迹，慢慢看就接近那些决定。生成图也有无数细节，但没人决定它们。看九分钟，常常什么也没有，或者只看到训练数据。这不是放弃的理由，而是任务：看自己的片子三分钟，问哪里是有意的。如果答案是没有，那就是你下一步要改的。", "section": "Story 1 · Looking", "source": ""}, {"number": 17, "key": "slide-17", "title": "See. Think. Wonder.", "script": "Let's practise. This routine comes from Project Zero at Harvard's school of education, and teachers use it with children. Three questions. What do you see? Only facts. What do you think about that? Now you may interpret. What does it make you wonder? Look at this still from a recent film for one full minute in silence. I will time it. Then talk with your neighbour for two minutes. Do not say whether you like it. Liking comes last.", "zh": "来练习。这个方法来自哈佛教育学院的 Project Zero。三个问题：你看到什么（只说事实）？你怎么想？它让你好奇什么？看这张电影剧照，安静一分钟，我计时。然后和旁边的人聊两分钟。不要说喜不喜欢，喜欢放在最后。", "section": "Story 1 · Looking", "source": "Routine: Project Zero, Harvard Graduate School of Education. Still: A Face Only a Mother Could Love, Robert Gaudette, 2026."}, {"number": 18, "key": "slide-18", "title": "A critic’s four steps, on a work you know: Edmond de Belamy", "script": "The classic method comes from Edmund Feldman, and it is simply four steps, in order. Describe: what's there, no opinions. A blurred man, a dark coat, a white collar, a pale empty background, and instead of a signature, a mathematical formula. Analyze: how is it built? The head sits high in the frame, almost cut off. The palette and the brushwork look like an Old Master, but no edge holds. Interpret: what does it mean? This GAN saw around fifteen thousand portraits. It paints the idea of a portrait, and it can't hold on to one person. That's actually a nice thought. Judge, at the very end. I find it interesting as an idea and dull as a picture. Notice I had to do three steps before I'm allowed an opinion. Most comments on the internet start and end at step four.", "zh": "经典方法来自 Feldman，四步，按顺序。描述：有什么，不带观点。模糊的男人、深色外套、白领子、空白背景，签名处是数学公式。分析：怎么构成的。头很靠上几乎被切掉，古典大师的色调和笔触，但没有一条边守得住。解释：什么意思。GAN看了约一万五千张肖像，画出的是“肖像”这个概念，留不住一个人。判断放最后：我觉得想法有趣，画很无聊。网上的评论大多从第四步开始，也在第四步结束。", "section": "Story 1 · Looking", "source": "Method: E. B. Feldman, Varieties of Visual Experience. Work: Obvious, 2018; sold at Christie’s, October 2018; dataset figure as reported by the artists."}, {"number": 19, "key": "slide-19", "title": "“Every AI image is an infographic about the dataset”", "script": "Now here's what is new about looking at AI work. Eryk Salvaggio teaches a course called Critical Topics: AI Images, and he starts with a map. John Snow's 1854 map of cholera in London. Each black mark is a death, and you read the picture to find the data. Salvaggio says every AI image is like that map. It is an infographic about the dataset. Here is his example: DALL-E asked for a bland stock photo of two people kissing. It looks awkward. Why? He went and looked at the training data for kissing, and found stock photographs, where actors are sitting together and asked to kiss. There's no emotion in the data, so there's none in the picture. Hamilton College now has a whole art history course built on this idea.", "zh": "AI作品的新东西在这里。Salvaggio 开课时先讲一张地图：1854年 John Snow 的伦敦霍乱图，每个黑点是一个死者，你读图来读数据。他说每张AI图都是关于数据集的信息图。例子：让 DALL·E 画两个人接吻，很尴尬。为什么？他去看训练数据，发现都是库存照片，演员被要求接吻。数据里没有情感，图里也没有。Hamilton 学院现在有整门艺术史课基于这个想法。", "section": "Story 1 · Looking", "source": "Eryk Salvaggio, “How to Read an AI Image”, and Critical Topics: AI Images (Bradley University, 2023; recommended by Harvard metaLAB’s AI Pedagogy Project). Hamilton College ARTH-208 opens with the same definition."}, {"number": 20, "key": "slide-20", "title": "How to read an AI image: four questions to add to your crit", "script": "So add these four questions to your critique. What is here too often? Golden light, a centred face, blurry backgrounds. That is the data speaking, not the artist. What's missing? Ask for a family, a doctor, a Hong Kong street, and notice who never shows up. Where does it break? Hands, text, reflections. A break shows you what the model never needed to learn. And the most important one for you as a maker: what did the artist fight? Which part of this work goes against the default? That is where the authorship lives. When you did the reverse engineering for Assignment 2, you were already doing this.", "zh": "加四个问题。什么出现得太多？金色光线、居中的脸、虚化背景，那是数据在说话。缺了什么？让它画家庭、医生、香港街道，看谁从不出现。哪里坏了？手、文字、反光，坏的地方说明模型从不需要学。最重要的：艺术家在对抗什么？哪里违背默认？作者性就在那里。作业2的逆向工程其实已经在做这件事。", "section": "Story 1 · Looking", "source": "Questions adapted from Salvaggio, “How to Read an AI Image”."}, {"number": 21, "key": "slide-21", "title": "Leave a model alone and it drifts to twelve pictures", "script": "This default is now measurable. Arend Hintze's group played a game of telephone between two AIs. Stable Diffusion draws a picture, another model describes it, and that description becomes the next prompt. A hundred rounds. They started from a hundred very different prompts. And no matter where they started, the images drifted to about twelve themes. Gothic cathedrals, natural landscapes, sports, stormy lighthouses. The reason is simple. That's what people chose to photograph and upload. Hintze says AI is really good at the first part of creativity, and really bad at the second part. The second part means selecting what's worth keeping. That's your job. Hito Steyerl has a name for these pictures. She calls them mean images. Mean as in average, and mean as in cruel.", "zh": "这种默认现在可以测量。Hintze 组让两个AI玩传话：SDXL画图，另一个模型描述，描述当下一轮提示词，一百轮。从一百个很不同的提示词出发，都漂到大约十二个主题：哥特教堂、自然风景、体育、风暴灯塔。原因很简单：这些是人们爱拍爱传的。Hintze说AI很擅长第一步，很不擅长第二步，也就是选择什么值得留下。那是你的工作。Steyerl 称之为 mean images：平均，也是刻薄。", "section": "Story 1 · Looking", "source": "Hintze et al., “Autonomous language-image generation loops converge to generic visual motifs”, Patterns, December 2025. 100 prompts; 4 image models and 4 description models tested."}, {"number": 22, "key": "slide-22", "title": "Useful: which crit method for which moment?", "script": "Here are four tools, and when to use each. See, Think, Wonder for meeting a new work. Feldman's four steps when you write. Stanford's I Like, I Wish, What If for a classmate's draft. It is the fastest of all. Liz Lerman, the choreographer, invented the Critical Response Process, and I love it because the artist goes first. First the audience says what was meaningful. Then the artist asks the questions they want answered. Then the audience asks neutral questions. Opinions come only at the end, and only with permission. On November 17 and 24, for the final presentations, we will use the last two. You will ask us first. And please, an I wish is not an insult, as long as it comes with a what if.", "zh": "四个工具。见到新作品用 See-Think-Wonder；写评论用 Feldman 四步；同学的草稿用斯坦福 d.school 的 I Like, I Wish, What If，最快。编舞家 Liz Lerman 的 Critical Response Process 我最喜欢，因为艺术家先开口：观众先说哪里有意义，艺术家提问，观众问中性问题，最后才在获得同意后给意见。11月17、24日期末展示就用后两种。", "section": "Story 1 · Looking", "source": "Lerman & Borstel, Critical Response Process (2003). Stanford d.school method “I Like, I Wish, What If”."}, {"number": 23, "key": "slide-23", "title": "If the label can change your number, was your number ever about the work?", "script": "Two minutes with your neighbour. If a label can change your number, was your number ever about the work? And a practical version: should a jury be told how a work was made before they judge it, or only after? The Colorado judges did not know, and they said it would not have changed their minds. Think about your own answer, because in the next story, machines are going to give their number.", "zh": "和旁边的人讨论两分钟。如果标签能改变你的分数，那分数还是关于作品的吗？实际一点：评审该在评判之前还是之后知道作品怎么做的？科罗拉多的评委当时不知道，事后说不会改变决定。想想你的答案，下一个故事轮到机器打分。", "section": "Story 1 · Looking", "source": ""}, {"number": 24, "key": "slide-24", "title": "Measuring", "script": "Story two is the technical part of tonight. You read papers and press releases that say: our model scored 8 percent better. Better at what? In this story I'll explain how these numbers are made, with slides borrowed from CMU and Stanford, and with some experiments we ran this afternoon. No heavy mathematics. You just need to know enough to not be fooled.", "zh": "第二个故事是今晚的技术部分。论文和新闻稿常说：我们的模型好了8%。好在哪？这里讲这些数字怎么来，用CMU和斯坦福的幻灯片，加上今天下午做的实验。不用重数学，只要够你不被骗。", "section": "Story 2 · Measuring", "source": ""}, {"number": 25, "key": "slide-25", "title": "Stanford’s first question: what is the task you care about?", "script": "Stefano Ermon opens this topic at Stanford with a very good question. What is the task you care about? If you build a classifier, it is easy: there's a right answer, and you count mistakes. A generator has no right answer. His slide says humans are the gold standard, but slow, biased and hard to repeat. And look at this line: memorizing the training set would give excellent samples, but it's clearly undesirable. Please hold on to that sentence. A plagiarist gets excellent marks on many of these tests.", "zh": "斯坦福 Ermon 这样开头：你关心的任务是什么？分类器有正确答案，数错误就行；生成模型没有正确答案。他的幻灯片说：人是金标准，但慢、有偏见、难重复。还有一句：记住训练集也能得到极好的样本，但这显然不是我们要的。记住这句，抄袭者在很多测试上都能拿高分。", "section": "Story 2 · Measuring", "source": "Stefano Ermon, Stanford CS236 “Deep Generative Models”, Lecture 15 “Evaluating Generative Models” (Fall 2023), shown with credit."}, {"number": 26, "key": "slide-26", "title": "Three ways to score generated video", "script": "There are basically three ways to score. One: compare with reality. Either compare pixels, or compare features, what a neural network sees. Two: ask another model. Show it the video and ask, is the horse on the grass? Three: ask people. Show them two videos and ask which they prefer, then compute a ranking. All three answer a smaller question than, is it good. Let's try the first one on a painting you know.", "zh": "基本三种打分方式。一，和真实比：比像素，或比特征（神经网络看到的）。二，问另一个模型：马在草地上吗？三，问人：两段视频选哪个，再算排名。三种都比“好不好”窄。先用你认识的画试试第一种。", "section": "Story 2 · Measuring", "source": "Structure follows Jun-Yan Zhu, CMU 16-726 Lecture 20, and Huang et al., VBench (CVPR 2024)."}, {"number": 27, "key": "slide-27", "title": "Game: be the metric. Rank B to G, closest to A first.", "script": "Here is a game. A is Van Gogh's Starry Night. I made six versions. B is the same image, moved eight pixels to the right. Can you see it? C is mirrored. D is compressed with very heavy JPEG. E is blurred. F has its colours changed. G is cut into squares and shuffled. Now you are the metric. Which one is closest to the original? Which is furthest? Take twenty seconds, and I'll ask for a show of hands for B, then D, then E.", "zh": "玩个游戏。A是《星夜》。我做了六个版本：B右移8像素，你看得出吗？C镜像，D重度JPEG压缩，E模糊，F改颜色，G切块打乱。现在你是指标。哪个最接近原作？哪个最远？想二十秒，然后举手。", "section": "Story 2 · Measuring", "source": "Vincent van Gogh, The Starry Night, 1889. Public domain. Variants made for this class."}, {"number": 28, "key": "slide-28", "title": "What pixels say: moving 8 pixels is worse than wrecking it", "script": "This is what we measured this afternoon. PSNR is the oldest score in the book. It takes each pixel and compares it with the same pixel in the original. Look at the dark red bar. B, the version moved by eight pixels, which you can't tell apart from the original, gets 16.6 decibels. The heavily damaged JPEG gets 22. The blurry one gets 20. So according to PSNR, wrecking the picture is better than moving it a tiny bit. SSIM, the more modern pixel score, says the same thing, even more strongly. Your visual system doesn't work like this. It doesn't care where exactly a pixel is. So if a paper reports PSNR for video, be careful about what it means.", "zh": "这是今天下午测的。PSNR 是最老的指标，逐像素和原图比。看深红色的 B：移动8像素，你根本看不出，只有16.6分贝；严重受损的JPEG有22，模糊的有20。按PSNR，毁掉画面比挪一点更好。SSIM 也一样，更明显。你的视觉不这样工作，不在乎像素的精确位置。", "section": "Story 2 · Measuring", "source": "Measured for this class, 3 Nov 2026 (768 × 608 px). PSNR = 10 log₁₀(255² / mean squared pixel error)."}, {"number": 29, "key": "slide-29", "title": "Features are smarter. A network still sees a lot of “same”.", "script": "So researchers stopped comparing pixels and started comparing features: what a neural network sees. Here we use CLIP. Now the shifted and mirrored versions are almost perfect, 0.997 and 0.998. That's much more like us. But look at the bottom. The scrambled one, cut into squares, the painting destroyed, still gets 0.87. Right colours, right brush texture, so to the network, it is still rather like Starry Night. And mirroring a painting matters a lot to a painter. The swirl now runs the other way, and the composition reads in the opposite direction. The network hardly cares. Better, but it isn't looking the way Jennifer Roberts looks.", "zh": "所以研究者改比特征，也就是网络看到的。这里用CLIP。移动和镜像几乎完美，0.997、0.998，更像人了。但打乱的仍有0.87：颜色和笔触对，画没了。镜像对画家很重要，旋涡方向和构图都反了，网络几乎不在乎。好一些，但它不是 Roberts 那样的看。", "section": "Story 2 · Measuring", "source": "Measured for this class, 3 Nov 2026, using CLIP ViT-L/14 image embeddings (cosine similarity)."}, {"number": 30, "key": "slide-30", "title": "Under the hood: FID, the number in every paper", "script": "Now the number you'll find in nearly every paper: FID, Fréchet Inception Distance. Here's Jun-Yan's slide. Take thousands of real images and pass them through a network. Each image becomes a point, so you get a cloud of points. Do the same with thousands of generated images. Fit a simple blob to each cloud, and measure how far apart the blobs are. Lower is better. That's all of it. Notice something important for artists. FID judges a crowd. It never looks at any one image. There is no such thing as the FID of your film.", "zh": "几乎每篇论文都有的数字：FID。这是朱俊彦的幻灯片。几千张真实图过网络，每张变成一个点，形成点云。生成图也一样。各拟合一个简单的团，量两团的距离，越小越好。注意：FID 评判的是一群图，从不看某一张。不存在“你这部电影的FID”。", "section": "Story 2 · Measuring", "source": "Slide from Jun-Yan Zhu, CMU 16-726, Lecture 20 “Evaluating Generative Models” (Spring 2025), shown with credit."}, {"number": 31, "key": "slide-31", "title": "Worked example: the copycat gets a perfect score", "script": "Here's a small example I computed, in two dimensions so we can see it. Grey is the real data, orange is the model. On the left, a copycat model that only replays its training set. The distance is 0.00, a perfect score. In the middle, a model making genuinely new things, 0.05, slightly worse. On the right, a model with one idea, always making the same kind of thing. That one gets punished, 1.00. So FID does see boring models. But notice: the plagiarist beats the original artist. That's what Stanford's slide warned us about. And as an artist, you want to be somewhere strange, off the cloud. FID calls that a mistake.", "zh": "我算的小例子，二维方便看。灰色是真实数据，橙色是模型。左边只会重放训练集的抄袭者，距离0.00，满分。中间真正做新东西的模型，0.05，略差。右边只有一个想法的模型，1.00，被罚。所以FID能看出无聊的模型，但抄袭者赢过了原创者。作为艺术家，你想待在云团外的奇怪地方，FID把这叫错误。", "section": "Story 2 · Measuring", "source": "Toy example in 2D, computed for this class with the Fréchet formula. Grey = real, orange = generated. Real FID uses about 2,048 dimensions and 10,000+ images."}, {"number": 32, "key": "slide-32", "title": "CMU: the same photo, three FIDs. Nothing visible changed.", "script": "Jun-Yan's lecture also shows how fragile this number is. Same images, saved as JPEG at quality 90: FID 6. At quality 75, which your eye can hardly see: FID 21. Even the choice of resize function in your code moves it from 2.68 to 2.99. And people celebrate papers that improve FID by less than that. His summary is funny. FID lovers say: our model improves from 6.8 to 6.75. FID haters say: stop using metrics and just look at the pixels. His advice: use the metrics, carefully, use several, and test on what you actually want to do.", "zh": "朱俊彦还展示了这个数字多脆弱。同样的图，存成JPEG质量90，FID 6；质量75，眼睛几乎看不出，FID 21。代码里换个缩放函数，从2.68变到2.99。而论文常庆祝比这还小的进步。他的总结很有趣：FID爱好者说从6.8提升到6.75，反对者说别用指标了直接看。他的建议：小心地用，多用几种，在真实任务上测。", "section": "Story 2 · Measuring", "source": "Slide from Jun-Yan Zhu, CMU 16-726, Lecture 20 “Evaluating Generative Models” (Spring 2025), shown with credit."}, {"number": 33, "key": "slide-33", "title": "Video makes it worse: a broken clip beats a clean one", "script": "For video there's FVD, FID's cousin. And here's a very useful slide. Three clips of clouds. The middle one has slightly damaged frames, but the motion is correct. The one on the right has good frames, but the motion is badly broken. A human would say the right one is much worse. FVD gives the right one the better score, 311 against 317. So our main video metric mostly judges frames. It's nearly blind to time. And time is what makes it a film. This is the same trap I showed you in Lecture 8, when a model beat real video on a lip-sync score.", "zh": "视频有 FVD，FID的亲戚。这页很有用：三段云的视频。中间帧略有损坏但运动正确；右边帧很好但运动严重破坏。人会说右边差得多，FVD却给右边更好的分：311比317。主流视频指标主要评判帧，几乎看不见时间。而时间才是电影。这和第8讲模型超过真视频的唇同步分数是同一个陷阱。", "section": "Story 2 · Measuring", "source": "Slide from Jun-Yan Zhu, CMU 16-726, Lecture 20 “Evaluating Generative Models” (Spring 2025), shown with credit. Ge et al., “On the Content Bias in Fréchet Video Distance”, CVPR 2024."}, {"number": 34, "key": "slide-34", "title": "VBench: stop giving one number. Give sixteen.", "script": "So people tried a better design. VBench, from CVPR 2024, says: stop giving one number. Split video quality into sixteen dimensions, things like temporal flicker, motion smoothness, keeping a subject's identity, colour, and spatial relationships. For each one, prepare about a hundred prompts, and check the measure against what people actually prefer. Now you get this radar shape instead of a rank. I like this very much. It's what a good critic does. Not, this is a seven. Instead: strong motion, weak hands.", "zh": "于是有更好的设计。VBench（CVPR 2024）说：别只给一个数，拆成16个维度：闪烁、运动平滑、身份保持、颜色、空间关系等。每个维度约100条提示词，并和人的偏好比对。得到的是雷达图而不是排名。我很喜欢，这就是好评论家的做法：不说这是7分，而说运动强、手弱。", "section": "Story 2 · Measuring", "source": "Huang et al., VBench: Comprehensive Benchmark Suite for Video Generative Models, CVPR 2024. Figure from the project page."}, {"number": 35, "key": "slide-35", "title": "VBench-2.0: can you score creativity?", "script": "VBench 2.0 goes further. It says current models look plausible but aren't truly faithful. It has five dimensions: human fidelity, controllability, creativity, physics, and commonsense. It uses large vision-language models as the graders, which is approach number two: ask a model. This works well for physics. Does the ball fall down? I'm less sure about creativity. Here the word means diversity and composition. And think about commonsense. Most art I admire would fail a commonsense test. Would Duchamp pass? So be careful when a word from art gets borrowed by a benchmark. The word stays the same, and the meaning shrinks.", "zh": "VBench 2.0 更进一步：现有模型看着合理，但不够真实。五个维度：人体保真、可控性、创造力、物理、常识。用视觉语言大模型当评委，也就是第二种方法。物理很合适：球会往下掉吗？创造力我就没那么确定，这里指多样性和构图。常识呢？我欣赏的艺术大多过不了常识测试。杜尚能过吗？艺术词被借去做指标时，词没变，意义缩小了。", "section": "Story 2 · Measuring", "source": "Huang et al., VBench-2.0 (2025). Figure from the project page."}, {"number": 36, "key": "slide-36", "title": "Asking people: the Elo ranking, from chess to video", "script": "Approach three: ask people. Show them two videos made from the same prompt, without saying which model made which, and let them pick. Then use the Elo system, invented for chess. If you beat someone stronger, you gain more points. There's one formula on the slide and you don't need to memorize it, you only need to feel what it means. A gap of 100 Elo points means you'd win about 64 percent of votes. A gap of 43 points means 56 percent. That's nearly a coin flip. Keep that in mind for the next slide.", "zh": "第三种：问人。同一提示词的两段视频，不告诉哪个模型，选一个，再用国际象棋的 Elo 算分。公式不用背，只要有感觉：差100分约赢64%的票，差43分约赢56%，几乎是抛硬币。记住这个，看下一页。", "section": "Story 2 · Measuring", "source": "Slide from Jun-Yan Zhu, CMU 16-726, Lecture 20 “Evaluating Generative Models” (Spring 2025), shown with credit. Numbers computed from the formula."}, {"number": 37, "key": "slide-37", "title": "This week’s top 10: first and tenth are 43 points apart", "script": "Here's the leaderboard as of today, from Artificial Analysis. Wan 3.0 at the top, 1173, then Utopai, MiniMax, FLUX, HeyGen, Seedance. Number ten is Gemini Omni Flash, 1130. The press writes: Wan is number one. But first and tenth are only 43 points apart. In a blind vote, the number one beats the number ten only 56 percent of the time. And this measures the model on general prompts. So don't pick your tool by its rank. Take your actual shot, the one you care about, run it on three of these models, and look.", "zh": "今天 Artificial Analysis 的榜单：Wan 3.0 第一，1173；第十是 Gemini Omni Flash，1130。媒体写 Wan 第一，但第一和第十只差43分，盲投中第一只赢第十56%。而且这是通用提示词。所以别按名次选工具：拿你真正在乎的镜头，在三个模型上跑，自己看。", "section": "Story 2 · Measuring", "source": "Artificial Analysis, Text-to-Video leaderboard (AA-Video-T2V v2.1), read 3 Nov 2026. Win rate computed from the Elo formula."}, {"number": 38, "key": "slide-38", "title": "What does a blind A/B vote reward?", "script": "Let me say something about what a vote like this rewards. People look for a few seconds and pick. So it rewards sharpness, glossy light, spectacle, and the most obvious reading of the prompt. It can't see a slow build, an ending, or a joke which lands late. And these votes don't only rank models, they can also train them. In our own lab, Harold Chen and colleagues published PhysHPO last year. It teaches a video model preferences at four levels, from the whole clip down to the motion, in order to make physics more plausible. It worked. But it taught me a lesson: what you reward is what you get. So where exactly did the taste of today's models come from? I'll show you. It's a very strange story.", "zh": "这种投票奖励什么？人只看几秒就选，所以奖励清晰、光泽、奇观和对提示词最明显的理解。它看不到缓慢铺垫、结尾、晚到的笑点。投票不仅排名，还能训练模型。我们组 Harold Chen 等人去年发表的 PhysHPO，在四个层次上教模型偏好，让物理更合理，有效。教训是：你奖励什么就得到什么。那今天模型的品味从哪来？故事很奇怪。", "section": "Story 2 · Measuring", "source": "Chen, Huang, Chen, Yang & Lim, “Hierarchical Fine-grained Preference Optimization for Physically Plausible Video Generation”, NeurIPS 2025 (arXiv 2508.10858)."}, {"number": 39, "key": "slide-39", "title": "How “taste” got into Stable Diffusion", "script": "In 2022, the LAION group wanted a better training set. So they built an aesthetic predictor. They asked people: how much do you like this image, from one to ten. They used about a quarter of a million photos from DPChallenge, a photography contest website, plus a set of AI images and logos. Then they trained a very small model on top of CLIP to predict that rating. They ran it across billions of internet images, and kept those scoring five or higher: 600 million. And the team behind Stable Diffusion version 1 used that subset for training. So the taste of some members of an online photo contest, squeezed into one small linear model, was built into a model used by millions of people. Even funnier: they chose the linear version partly because its results looked best to them.", "zh": "2022年 LAION 想要更好的训练集，于是做了美学预测器：问人“1到10分多喜欢这张图”，用了约25万张摄影比赛网站 DPChallenge 的照片，加上AI图和logo。在CLIP上训练一个很小的模型来预测分数，跑遍几十亿张网图，留下5分以上的6亿张。Stable Diffusion v1 用这部分训练。于是一个摄影比赛网站部分用户的品味，压缩进一个小线性模型，进了数百万人使用的模型。更有趣的是，他们选线性版本部分是因为结果在他们看来最好看。", "section": "Story 2 · Measuring", "source": "LAION, “LAION-Aesthetics” (blog). Ratings from SAC (176k), LAION-Logos (15k), AVA / dpchallenge.com (~250k)."}, {"number": 40, "key": "slide-40", "title": "We ran that predictor on art history this afternoon", "script": "So this afternoon I downloaded an open copy of that predictor and ran it on works from tonight. I want to be clear, this is one reproduction of each work, so it's a demonstration and not a study. Copley gets 6.15, Van Gogh 5.88, fine. Duchamp's urinal gets 5.26. It passes. Now look at the cut-off line. The DALL-E picture, whose prompt literally asked for a soulless, bland stock photo, lands right on the line at 4.997. And Vera Molnár's plotter drawing, in the V&A right now, gets 4.87. It falls below. Same for John Snow's map. Under this rule a bland stock photo is better than Vera Molnár. By this one score, a drawing like that would have been left out of the training data. I find that a very good reason to look at your defaults.", "zh": "今天下午我下载了这个预测器，跑了今晚的作品。先说清楚：每件作品只是一张复制品，这是演示不是研究。科普利6.15，梵高5.88，杜尚的小便池5.26，过线。看分界线：提示词明确写“无灵魂、平淡的库存照片”的DALL·E图，正好压线，4.997。而现在在V&A展出的莫尔纳绘图是4.87，线下。John Snow的地图也是。按此规则，平淡库存照比莫尔纳好。按这个分数，这样的绘图会被排除在训练数据之外。这是审视默认值的好理由。", "section": "Story 2 · Measuring", "source": "Our run, 3 Nov 2026: LAION improved aesthetic predictor (sac+logos+ava1, ViT-L/14) on one reproduction of each work. A demonstration, not a study."}, {"number": 41, "key": "slide-41", "title": "And the score prefers my pink Van Gogh", "script": "One more, because it's too good. On the left, Van Gogh's Starry Night, 5.88. On the right, I rotated the hue with two lines of code. It took about twenty seconds. 6.15. My pink version beats Van Gogh. I should say the predictor isn't random. When I blur the painting, it drops to 4.86, so it's measuring something real. But it's measuring what a group of people, rating images on a website, tend to click high. That's a fact about taste in one place and time. It isn't a fact about Van Gogh.", "zh": "再来一个。左边《星夜》5.88。右边我用两行代码转了色相，二十秒，6.15。我的粉色版赢了梵高。预测器不是随机的：模糊后降到4.86，确实测到了东西。但它测的是网站上一群人倾向打高分的东西，那是关于某时某地品味的事实，不是关于梵高的事实。", "section": "Story 2 · Measuring", "source": "Our run, 3 Nov 2026, same predictor. A blurred version scores 4.86, so the predictor is not random. It just isn’t Van Gogh’s audience."}, {"number": 42, "key": "slide-42", "title": "The loop: why everything starts to look the same", "script": "Put it all together and you get a loop. People rate things. Ratings become a score. The score tunes the model. The model fills the feed. And people, trained by the feed, rate things again. There is an old law for this from economics, Goodhart's law: when a measure becomes a target, it stops being a good measure. Hintze's twelve pictures are what the end of the loop looks like. This is the technical reason that your own decisions matter more each year. The more the default converges, the more valuable it is to deviate from it.", "zh": "合起来是一个循环：人打分，分数变成指标，指标调模型，模型填满信息流，被信息流训练的人再打分。经济学有古德哈特定律：指标一旦成为目标，就不再是好指标。Hintze 的十二张图就是循环的尽头。这是你的决定越来越重要的技术原因：默认越收敛，偏离越有价值。", "section": "Story 2 · Measuring", "source": "Goodhart (1975), phrasing after M. Strathern (1997). Hintze et al., Patterns (2025)."}, {"number": 43, "key": "slide-43", "title": "Useful: reading a leaderboard or a press release", "script": "Here's your practical checklist. Whenever you see a new number, ask six things. Which task? Who graded it? How big is the gap, really? Does the metric even see time? Would a copycat score well? And then run your own test. Take your three hardest shots, three models, and use your own eyes. That takes one evening and it's worth more than any leaderboard. Metrics are good for one thing above all: comparing versions of your own pipeline. They're terrible for deciding what's good.", "zh": "实用清单：看到新数字问六件事。什么任务？谁评的？差距多大？指标看得见时间吗？抄袭者能得高分吗？然后自己测：三个最难的镜头、三个模型、自己的眼睛。一晚上，比任何榜单都值。指标最适合比较自己流程的版本，最不适合决定什么是好。", "section": "Story 2 · Measuring", "source": ""}, {"number": 44, "key": "slide-44", "title": "You generated 100 takes. Would you let a score pick the top ten for you to look at?", "script": "One minute before the break. You generated a hundred takes. Would you let a score choose the ten you actually look at? Many professionals already do this. What do you save, and what do you lose? Tell your neighbour. Then we go to student presentations.", "zh": "休息前一分钟。你生成了100条，愿意让分数选出你真正要看的十条吗？很多专业人士已经这样做。省了什么，丢了什么？告诉旁边的人，然后是学生展示。", "section": "Story 2 · Measuring", "source": ""}, {"number": 45, "key": "slide-45", "title": "Break: student presentations", "script": "Now it's time for this week's student presentations. Please practise tonight's tool. For each presenter, give one I like, one I wish, and one what if. Then a ten-minute break, and we go to Story 3.", "zh": "现在是学生展示。请练习今晚的工具：对每位同学说一个 I like、一个 I wish、一个 what if。然后休息十分钟，进入第三个故事。", "section": "Story 2 · Measuring", "source": ""}, {"number": 46, "key": "slide-46", "title": "Showing", "script": "Welcome back. Story three is about showing. This has been a remarkable year: a museum of AI art opened in Los Angeles, a city museum in Wisconsin received a protest letter with over 900 signatures, a pioneer of computer art opened at the V&A two weeks ago, and Cannes and the Oscars drew lines around AI while an AI short won at Venice. Let's go through these, and use our new tools as we go.", "zh": "欢迎回来。第三个故事讲展示。今年不寻常：洛杉矶开了AI艺术博物馆，威斯康星一家美术馆收到900多人签名的抗议信，计算机艺术先驱两周前在V&A开展，戛纳和奥斯卡划线，而一部AI短片在威尼斯得奖。逐个看，同时用上刚学的工具。", "section": "Story 3 · Showing", "source": "Installation view, Machine Dreams: Rainforest, DATALAND, Los Angeles. Refik Anadol Studio, via NPR."}, {"number": 47, "key": "slide-47", "title": "First, two kinds of work that often get mixed up", "script": "First a distinction which curators use, and which saves a lot of confusion. There is art made with AI, where the model is the brush, like Dataland. And there's art about AI, where the model is the subject. This summer, the Schirn museum in Frankfurt showed The World Through AI, with Hito Steyerl, Trevor Paglen, and Kate Crawford and Vladan Joler's map of the labour inside an Amazon Echo. Many of those works hardly use generative AI at all. The strongest pieces are often both. So ask yourself which one you are making. If you are making art about AI, your audience has to know how it was made. If you are making art with AI, maybe they don't.", "zh": "先区分两类。用AI做的艺术，模型是画笔，比如 Dataland。关于AI的艺术，模型是主题。今夏法兰克福 Schirn 的《透过AI看世界》有 Steyerl、Paglen、Crawford 和 Joler。很多作品几乎不用生成式AI。最强的往往两者都是。问问自己在做哪一种：关于AI，观众必须知道怎么做的；用AI做，也许不必。", "section": "Story 3 · Showing", "source": "Schirn Kunsthalle Frankfurt, 11 June – 20 Sept 2026 (about 40 works by about 30 artists). Image right: Post Generation, tiat, San Francisco, ARTnews, Oct 2026."}, {"number": 48, "key": "slide-48", "title": "Dataland: “the world’s first museum of AI arts”", "script": "Dataland opened on the twentieth of June in Los Angeles, inside a Frank Gehry development. It has 25,000 square feet and five galleries. Refik Anadol's studio trained what it calls the Large Nature Model, on data from museums, labs, and travels to the Amazon. You wear a wristband, the room reads your body, and the images respond. There's a 200-channel sound system, there's scent, there's even taste. NPR calls it sensory overload. If you have seen his work, you know the look. Now, let's use our tools. If you were writing a crit, what would you say? Let's see what the critics said.", "zh": "Dataland 6月20日在洛杉矶盖里设计的建筑里开幕，2.5万平方英尺，五个展厅。Anadol 工作室用博物馆、实验室和亚马逊的数据训练“大自然模型”。戴上腕带，房间读取身体信号，图像响应。200声道音响，还有气味和味道。NPR 说是感官超载。如果你写点评，会说什么？看评论家怎么说。", "section": "Story 3 · Showing", "source": "Artsy, 22 June 2026; NPR, 29 July 2026. Installation view: Refik Anadol Studio. Video: Revista ISP (Spanish). (“World’s first” is the museum’s own claim.)"}, {"number": 49, "key": "slide-49", "title": "Read the critics: which question are they answering?", "script": "Here are three reviews. Ann Hirsch at Artnet says: a screensaver on steroids, that feels so real you could live inside it, and she means it as praise. She took her young kids, and her six-year-old wanted to go back every day. Laura Hertzfeld in The Art Newspaper found a sense of joy, something she feels has been lost from museum tech shows. R. Daniel Foster in Forbes calls it an entertainment complex designed to astonish. And Jerry Saltz famously called Anadol's earlier work at MoMA a sort of lava lamp. Now look at the words. Screensaver, joy, entertainment, lava lamp. None of these are about whether it's AI. They're arguments about what a museum is for. That's a real critical debate, and it's question two, not question one.", "zh": "三篇评论。Artnet 的 Hirsch：打了激素的屏保，真实得能住进去，这是赞美；她六岁的孩子天天想回去。《艺术新闻》的 Hertzfeld：找回了科技展失去的快乐。《福布斯》的 Foster：为了让人惊叹而设计的娱乐综合体。Saltz 曾说 Anadol 在MoMA的作品像熔岩灯。看这些词：屏保、快乐、娱乐、熔岩灯，都不是在说是不是AI，而是在争美术馆是干什么的。这是第二个问题。", "section": "Story 3 · Showing", "source": "As quoted in The Week, 1 July 2026, and Artnet, June 2026."}, {"number": 50, "key": "slide-50", "title": "Museums make claims too. Which would you check?", "script": "Dataland also makes claims. The data is permission-based, from partners like the Smithsonian and Cornell. The model runs in a low-carbon data centre, 87 percent carbon-free. And each visit uses about the energy of one iPhone charge. I'm not saying these are false. We haven't checked them. But a good critic asks how you could check. Does the one iPhone count training, or only running? Does it include all those screens? Credit where it's due: Anadol names his sources and talks about energy, and most AI artists don't. That's a higher standard than the rest of the field. So hold everyone to it, including yourselves in your final projects.", "zh": "Dataland 也有声明：数据经过许可，来自史密森尼、康奈尔等；模型在低碳数据中心运行，87%无碳；每次参观约等于给iPhone充一次电。我不是说它们是假的，我们没查过。但好的评论者会问怎么查：一次充电算不算训练？算不算这些屏幕？公平地说，Anadol 公开数据来源、谈能耗，大多数AI艺术家没有。这是更高标准，所以请用同样标准要求所有人，包括你们自己。", "section": "Story 3 · Showing", "source": "Claims as reported by Artsy (22 June 2026), which notes they “will likely be closely watched”. We have not verified them."}, {"number": 51, "key": "slide-51", "title": "Madison, 18 September: a museum, Beeple, and a curtain", "script": "Let's go from Los Angeles to Madison, Wisconsin, six weeks ago. The Madison Museum of Contemporary Art opened Diffuse Control, by Beeple, the artist who sold a 69-million-dollar NFT. It has twelve synchronized screens. Visitors and curators can use their phones and a control panel to transform works from the museum's own collection through a diffusion model. The museum's director calls it a sculpture that moves. And a local artist, Noah Peterson, sent an open letter against the show, which more than 900 people signed. People called the decision disappointing and disgusting. This wasn't Hollywood or the Vatican. It's a medium-sized museum and its own community.", "zh": "从洛杉矶到威斯康星的麦迪逊，六周前。麦迪逊当代美术馆开幕《Diffuse Control》，作者是卖出6900万美元NFT的 Beeple。12块同步屏幕，观众和策展人用手机和控制台，通过扩散模型改造馆藏作品。馆长说这是一座会动的雕塑。当地艺术家 Noah Peterson 发公开信反对，900多人签名。这不是好莱坞或梵蒂冈，而是一家中型美术馆和自己的社区。", "section": "Story 3 · Showing", "source": "Wisconsin Public Radio, 25 Sept 2026. Photo: Angela Major / WPR. Video: Beeple, on YouTube; also on the museum’s page."}, {"number": 52, "key": "slide-52", "title": "Two sentences worth arguing about", "script": "Here are the two positions. The letter says: you did not get explicit consent from the artists whose work you fed in. And also: diffusion models are themselves trained on art scraped without consent. The director, Paul Baker Prindle, says: we have listened, and one can listen and hear, but also still not agree. And he says that blanket policies around artists are incredibly dangerous. I'd like you to notice something. The letter makes two complaints. They're different. Let me draw them.", "zh": "两种立场。公开信说：你们没有得到被放进系统的艺术家的明确同意；而且扩散模型本身就是用未经同意抓取的艺术训练的。馆长 Prindle 说：可以倾听、听见，但仍不同意；对艺术家设全面禁令非常危险。注意，这封信提出了两个不同的指控。我画出来。", "section": "Story 3 · Showing", "source": "Wisconsin Public Radio, 25 Sept 2026."}, {"number": 53, "key": "slide-53", "title": "Two layers of consent, and the museum answered one", "script": "Layer one is the collection, the works you see on the screen. The museum says it got consent where the law required it, from copyright holders of contemporary work, and that some older works are public domain. That can be checked, and it's answered. Layer two is the model. Behind any diffusion model are billions of images, and nobody in Madison can ask those artists. The museum can't answer that, because it isn't really a question for one museum. Does that mean no museum can ever show diffusion work? Or does it mean every museum using it has to say so on the wall? We will debate this at the end. Keep your position in mind.", "zh": "第一层是馆藏：屏幕上看到的作品。美术馆说在法律要求处取得了同意，部分老作品已进入公有领域。这可以核查，算回答了。第二层是模型：任何扩散模型背后都有数十亿张图，麦迪逊没人能去问那些艺术家。这不是一家美术馆能回答的问题。那是不是说美术馆永远不能展示扩散模型作品？还是必须在墙上说明？最后我们辩论。", "section": "Story 3 · Showing", "source": "Photo: Angela Major / WPR. Layer 2 image: LAION-Aesthetics blog. Director’s position as reported by WPR."}, {"number": 54, "key": "slide-54", "title": "Two weeks ago at the V&A: Vera Molnár", "script": "Now a very different show. Two weeks ago the V&A in London opened Degrees of Disruption, about Vera Molnár and generative art. Over fifty works, it's free, and it runs until May. Molnár was born in 1924 and died in 2023, at ninety-nine. She worked with computers and plotters from 1968, long before anybody took it seriously. She'd write a program which draws neat rows of lines, and then introduce a little randomness. She called this one percent of disorder. Look at this drawing. A rigid structure, and a tiny bit of trembling. Your eye keeps going to the places where the rule breaks. If you are ever in London, please go. It is the opposite of Dataland, and I think you should see both.", "zh": "另一个完全不同的展览。两周前V&A开幕《Degrees of Disruption》，关于莫尔纳和生成艺术，五十多件作品，免费，到明年5月。莫尔纳1924年生，2023年去世，99岁。1968年起就用电脑和绘图仪，远早于有人当回事。她写程序画整齐的线，再加一点随机，叫“百分之一的无序”。看这张：严格的结构，一点点颤动，眼睛总去规则破裂的地方。它是 Dataland 的反面，两个都该看。", "section": "Story 3 · Showing", "source": "V&A, South Kensington; opening date from art-magazine.ai listings. Image: V&A website. Video: Studio International, 2017. Shown for teaching and criticism."}, {"number": 55, "key": "slide-55", "title": "Same word, “generative”. Different decisions.", "script": "We use the same word, generative, for both of these. But look at where the decisions are. For Molnár, she writes the rule. Randomness is one percent, chosen and tuned. She can explain every line. And no default exists, because nobody has done this before. For a diffusion model the rule is learned from billions of images, noise is where everything starts, the artist can explain almost nothing, and the default is very strong. So where can you fight? Your dataset, like Anna Ridler from Lecture 5. Your prompts and seeds. Your edits. And above all, what you delete. Molnár's art was the design of constraints. Yours can be too. When you stand up on November 17, tell us your constraint. It's a very good first line.", "zh": "同一个词“生成”，决定在哪里却不同。莫尔纳自己写规则，随机是精选的1%，每条线都能解释，也没有默认，因为没人做过。扩散模型规则学自数十亿张图，噪声是起点，几乎解释不了，默认很强。你能在哪里抗争？数据集（第5讲的 Ridler）、提示词和种子、编辑，尤其是删掉什么。11月17日站上台，先告诉我们你的约束，这是很好的开场白。", "section": "Story 3 · Showing", "source": ""}, {"number": 56, "key": "slide-56", "title": "Last month: artists adopt a dead model", "script": "Here's one that connects to Lecture 7, when I asked what a work needs in order to outlive its tool. Last month in San Francisco, a show called Post Generation. Google's Veo 1 is now dead. No one can generate with it. A creative technologist named Tina Tarighian asked: what do we do with these leftover scraps of a model? And she gave part of the archive to three artists. Haley Mortin, who once worked as a data annotator labelling images for AI, noticed Veo 1's broken text, and knitted it into ten samplers, like the ones that young women stitched in the eighteenth century while learning to write. Karyn Nakamura projects the clips on cloth with printed research papers underneath. So an old model's mistakes become material. This model looked terrible, and that's exactly why it's interesting.", "zh": "这个连到第7讲：作品怎么比工具活得长。上月旧金山的《Post Generation》。Veo 1 已死，没人能再用。Google 的 Tina Tarighian 问：这些剩下的碎片能做什么？她把部分存档给了三位艺术家。曾做数据标注员的 Haley Mortin 注意到 Veo 1 乱码般的文字，织成十幅刺绣样本，就像18世纪年轻女性学写字时绣的那样。Karyn Nakamura 把片段投到布上，下面放打印的研究论文。旧模型的错误变成材料。", "section": "Story 3 · Showing", "source": "Shanti Escalante-De Mattei, “In Search of the Future, Artists Give New Life to Dead AI Models”, ARTnews, 12 Oct 2026. Photo: ARTnews."}, {"number": 57, "key": "slide-57", "title": "Film, 2026: the gates went up, and an AI short walked through another door", "script": "Now film. Look at this year. April 9th: Cannes says AI-driven films can't compete in the main competition, and its president says they refuse to let AI dictate the law. That same month, in that same town, the World AI Film Festival received around 5,500 entries. May 2nd: the Oscars say performances must be demonstrably performed by humans with their consent, and screenplays must be human-authored. June: Runway's festival gives its Grand Prix to a short by a man named Robert Gaudette. And in September, on the Lido in Venice, alongside but not part of the Venice Film Festival, the same film wins again, from a jury chaired by the Oscar-winning director Gabriele Salvatores. Two film worlds now, side by side. And we can look at this film.", "zh": "再看电影。4月9日戛纳：AI驱动的电影不能进主竞赛，主席说拒绝让AI制定规则。同月同城，世界AI电影节收到约5500部。5月2日奥斯卡：表演须由人真实完成且经同意，剧本须由人创作。6月 Runway 把大奖给了 Robert Gaudette 的短片。9月在威尼斯利多岛（与威尼斯电影节同期但不属于它），同一部片再次获奖，评审主席是奥斯卡得主 Salvatores。两个电影世界并排。来看这部片。", "section": "Story 3 · Showing", "source": "Agerpres, 9 Apr 2026; HackerNoon on WAiFF; TechCrunch, 2 May 2026; aif.runway.com; Reply press release, 7 Sept 2026 (held alongside, not part of, the Venice Film Festival)."}, {"number": 58, "key": "slide-58", "title": "A Face Only a Mother Could Love", "script": "It's called A Face Only a Mother Could Love. Robert Gaudette made it alone, with no actors and no crew, in 7 minutes and 50 seconds, using tools including Grok, Nano Banana and Seedance. It's about Marcel, a Parisian man with a disfigured face, who dances in his apartment every night, and walks through Paris with complete confidence as the world moves away from him. The Hollywood Reporter wrote that depending on your point of view, Gaudette is cinema's great hope, or its greatest threat. I want to try something the Hollywood Reporter can't. Let's do a real crit.", "zh": "片名《只有母亲才爱的脸》。Gaudette 一人完成，没有演员和剧组，7分50秒，用了 Grok、Nano Banana、Seedance 等。讲巴黎人 Marcel，脸有缺陷，每晚在公寓跳舞，自信地穿过巴黎，世界在躲开他。《好莱坞报道者》写：视角不同，他要么是电影的希望，要么是最大威胁。我们来做一次真正的点评。", "section": "Story 3 · Showing", "source": "aif.runway.com; Reply press release, 7 Sept 2026; Steven Zeitchik, The Hollywood Reporter, 17 June 2026. Still from the film."}, {"number": 59, "key": "slide-59", "title": "Live crit: watch 2 minutes, then four steps", "script": "Here's how we'll do it. I'll play the first two minutes. Then I want four volunteers, one for each step. Describe: only what's on the screen. Analyze: how is it cut, where's the camera, what repeats? Interpret: what is it about? And here is something I find interesting. A film with no actors, about a man whose face people won't look at. Is that an accident? Then judge, at the very end. Use the questions from earlier: what's the default here, and what did he fight? One rule. If you start with, it's just AI, you go back to step one. Same if you start with, it's amazing.", "zh": "这样做：我放前两分钟，然后四位同学各负责一步。描述：只说屏幕上有什么。分析：怎么剪、机位在哪、什么在重复。解释：讲什么？一部没有演员的电影，讲一个没人愿意看他脸的人，这是偶然吗？最后判断：默认在哪里，他对抗了什么？规则：谁一开口就说“不过是AI”，回到第一步；说“太棒了”也一样。", "section": "Story 3 · Showing", "source": ""}, {"number": 60, "key": "slide-60", "title": "Useful: write the wall text for your own piece", "script": "Here's something practical. Before you show anything, write the wall text. In a museum this small label is often the only thing people read. Four lines. A tombstone: title, name, year, and medium. One idea: what you want the audience to feel or notice. One sentence, with no adjectives. One constraint: the rule you set yourself, and what you refused to let the model do. And one honest line: which models, what data, whose references, and who agreed. Sixty words. If you can't write these four lines, you aren't ready to show the work.", "zh": "实用的：展出前先写墙上标签，美术馆里人们常常只读这个。四行。作品信息：标题、姓名、年份、媒介。一个想法：希望观众感受或注意到什么，一句话，不用形容词。一个约束：你给自己定的规则，不让模型做什么。一句实话：用了哪些模型、什么数据、谁的参考、谁同意了。60个词。写不出来，就还没准备好展出。", "section": "Story 3 · Showing", "source": "Example invented for this class."}, {"number": 61, "key": "slide-61", "title": "Go and see some: open now or soon", "script": "You should go and look at real work. Here's a list of what's open now or soon. The V&A, Dataland, Paris Photo's digital sector next week, the Whitney's history of internet art opening later this month, and Emi Kusano in Japan. And from our own university, the SURREALITY exhibition with HKUST Guangzhou, which is expected back in Hong Kong for public engagement; please check the dates. If you can't travel, many of these have good online material. Go and spend your nine minutes.", "zh": "去看真的作品。这是现在或即将开放的展：V&A、Dataland、下周巴黎摄影展数字单元、本月底惠特尼的网络艺术史、日本的 Emi Kusano，还有我们学校和港科大（广州）的 SURREALITY，据说会回香港，请查日期。去花你的九分钟。", "section": "Story 3 · Showing", "source": "Listings: art-magazine.ai, read 3 Nov 2026; HKUST press release, 16 June 2026. Please check each venue’s website before you travel."}, {"number": 62, "key": "slide-62", "title": "Signing", "script": "Story four: signing. A painter signs a painting, and that's a promise: I made this, I stand behind it. What does a signature mean now? Let me start with a story from last month. It's not about art at all, and it shows the whole problem.", "zh": "第四个故事：署名。画家签名是一种承诺：这是我做的，我负责。现在署名意味着什么？先讲上个月的事，和艺术无关，却展示了整个问题。", "section": "Story 4 · Signing", "source": "Our signed demo of Van Gogh’s The Starry Night; the card shows what its manifest says."}, {"number": 63, "key": "slide-63", "title": "Last month: a science video prize taken back", "script": "Nikon runs a famous science contest for microscope images, called Small World. In September it announced the video winner, a gorgeous film of cilia in lung tissue, with red, blue and purple structures. Then Nikon posted it on LinkedIn, and microscopists started looking. One said: those purple things look like mitochondria, but they're the size of a nucleus, and that doesn't exist in biology. Then a researcher found a SynthID watermark in the video. The maker said the footage was real, and AI was only used to colour the structures. Nikon re-examined it, and around October 9th it disqualified the entry and gave the prize to another film. One scientist, Melanie White, put it well: scientific images are data. Look at the order. Experts' eyes first. The watermark second. The rule third. Now, how does that watermark work?", "zh": "尼康有著名的显微影像比赛 Small World。9月宣布视频奖：肺组织纤毛，红蓝紫，很美。发到领英后显微镜专家开始看：紫色结构像线粒体，却有细胞核那么大，生物学上不存在。又有研究者在视频里发现 SynthID 水印。作者说素材是真的，AI只用来上色。尼康复审，约10月9日取消资格，奖给了另一部片。顺序：专家的眼睛，水印，规则。水印怎么工作？", "section": "Story 4 · Signing", "source": "Gizmodo, 2 Oct 2026; Newser, 9 Oct 2026; PetaPixel, 1 Oct 2026. The entrant says he believed he stayed within the guidelines."}, {"number": 64, "key": "slide-64", "title": "Under the hood: three layers, three different promises", "script": "There are three layers, and they make three different promises. The first is a label you can see: an AI-generated tag, or a line in the end credits. Its promise is only that someone told you. The second is signed metadata, called C2PA or Content Credentials. That's a record inside the file saying who made it, with what tool, and what edits, and it's cryptographically signed. The third is a pixel watermark, like Google's SynthID. It's a hidden pattern in the pixels themselves. Since May, OpenAI and Google both use two and three together, and YouTube labels AI video automatically. Let's try the second layer ourselves. This afternoon I signed a picture.", "zh": "三层，三种承诺。一，看得见的标签：只承诺有人告诉你了。二，签名元数据 C2PA：文件里记录谁、什么工具、什么编辑，并加密签名。三，像素水印如 SynthID：藏在像素里的图案。5月起 OpenAI 和谷歌都同时用二和三，YouTube 自动打标。我们试第二层，今天下午我签了一张图。", "section": "Story 4 · Signing", "source": "c2paviewer.com, May 2026 (OpenAI 19 May; YouTube 27 May; Google I/O). Google reports 100+ billion items marked with SynthID."}, {"number": 65, "key": "slide-65", "title": "Under the hood: C2PA in four steps", "script": "Here is how C2PA works. It's the same idea as the padlock in your browser. Step one: hash. Boil the bytes of the file down to one short fingerprint. Change one bit, and the fingerprint is completely different. Step two: claim. Write down facts: this was created, by a trained model, please don't use it for AI training, and here's the hash. Step three: sign, with your private key. Anyone with your certificate can check it came from you. Step four: put the whole thing inside the file. Now notice what this doesn't say. It doesn't say that the claim is true. A liar can sign a lie. It only says: this person said this about this exact file.", "zh": "C2PA 和浏览器的小锁是同一思路。一，哈希：文件字节压成短指纹，改一位全变。二，声明：写下事实，由训练模型创建、请勿用于训练，附上哈希。三，用私钥签名，有证书就能验证是你。四，把它放进文件。注意它没说什么：不说声明是真的，骗子也能签谎言。它只说：这个人对这个文件说了这句话。", "section": "Story 4 · Signing", "source": "C2PA Technical Specification; c2pa.org. Same public-key idea as the padlock in your browser."}, {"number": 66, "key": "slide-66", "title": "See it: the real manifest we signed this afternoon", "script": "Here's the actual output. I took Starry Night and signed it with a demo certificate I made myself. You can see it says action: created. Digital source type: trained algorithmic media, which is the standard phrase for made by AI. There's also this line, training and mining: not allowed. Your work can carry that request around, though nothing forces anybody to obey it. And the reader says one more interesting thing: signing credential untrusted. The mathematics is fine, but nobody has agreed to trust me as a signer. Trust isn't in the file. It's in a list of who you trust, and somebody controls that list. Now let's attack it.", "zh": "这是真实输出。我用自己做的演示证书签了《星夜》。写着：created，由训练模型生成；还有一行：不允许训练和挖掘。这只是请求，没人强制。读取器还说 signingCredential.untrusted：数学没问题，但没人同意信任我这个签名者。信任不在文件里，在一份名单里，而有人控制这份名单。来攻击它。", "section": "Story 4 · Signing", "source": "Our run, 3 Nov 2026, using c2pa-python 0.38. Output shortened."}, {"number": 67, "key": "slide-67", "title": "We broke it four ways, in Python, in five minutes", "script": "Here's what happened. The signed file reads valid. Then I re-saved it as a JPEG, one line of code. No manifest found. It's gone, no warning. I took a screenshot: gone. I resized it, as you would for upload: gone. Then I tried to cheat. I changed some pixels, but kept the manifest attached. And that one it caught: invalid, data hash mismatch. So here's the lesson. It's tamper evident. If you edit it and keep the record, it knows. But stripping it is trivial. So a credential proves a lot when it's there, and its absence proves nothing. Please never say: there's no credential, so it's fake. Most genuine photos in the world have none.", "zh": "结果：已签名文件有效。重存JPEG，一行代码：没有清单，消失了，没有任何警告。截屏：没了。缩放上传：没了。然后作弊：改像素、保留清单：无效，哈希不符，抓到了。教训：它能显示篡改，但去掉它极其容易。存在时能证明很多，不存在什么也证明不了。千万别说：没有凭证所以是假的，世界上大多数真实照片都没有。", "section": "Story 4 · Signing", "source": "Our run, 3 Nov 2026 (c2pa-python 0.38; res.json in the lecture materials). Platforms may keep or re-sign credentials on their own."}, {"number": 68, "key": "slide-68", "title": "Under the hood: the watermark. A hidden message, tested.", "script": "Now the third layer. The idea is to hide a message in the image's frequencies, where your eye isn't sensitive. I tested the open method which Stable Diffusion used, called DWT-DCT, on one image. Without changes it gets a hundred percent of the bits. But a normal JPEG save drops it to 67 percent, and 50 percent is a coin flip. Crop, rotate or blur, and it's nearly gone. Please don't conclude this about SynthID. SynthID is a learned watermark, built to survive this, and it's what caught the Nikon video. But it's closed, you can't test it the way we just did, and people keep publishing attacks, with disputed numbers. Remember, a watermark can only prove something about models that chose to use one. Open-weight models usually have no such requirement.", "zh": "第三层。把信息藏在频率里，眼睛不敏感的地方。我测了 SD 用过的开源方法 DWT-DCT，一张图。不改动100%，普通JPEG降到67%，50%就是抛硬币，裁剪旋转模糊几乎消失。别把这个结论套到 SynthID 上：它是学习出来的水印，更强，尼康事件就靠它。但它封闭，没法这样测，攻击的数字也有争议。而且水印只对选择使用它的模型有效。", "section": "Story 4 · Signing", "source": "Our run, 3 Nov 2026 (invisible-watermark library; 64-bit message on one image). A different and much weaker method than SynthID. Unmarked control: 50%."}, {"number": 69, "key": "slide-69", "title": "Where the rules are, today (this is not legal advice)", "script": "Here's a quick map, and please note I'm not a lawyer. In the EU, since August 2nd, deepfakes must be labelled by whoever publishes them. And on December 2nd, one month from now, the grace period ends for existing tools, which must then mark their outputs by machine. In mainland China, since September last year, there are both visible and hidden labels, and platforms ask creators to declare. So don't strip those labels. In the US, there's no federal labelling law, but the Supreme Court declined the Thaler case in March, leaving the rule that AI alone can't be an author. Hong Kong is interesting: our Copyright Ordinance already covers computer-generated works. The author is the person who made the arrangements. And for your career, the most immediate rules are festival rules. Read them first. Nikon's winner didn't think he had broken one.", "zh": "快速地图，我不是律师。欧盟：8月2日起深度伪造须由发布者标注；12月2日，也就是一个月后，现有工具的宽限期结束，须机器可读标记。中国大陆：去年9月起要求显式和隐式标识，平台要求创作者声明，别去掉。美国：无联邦标注法，最高法院3月拒审 Thaler，AI单独不能当作者。香港：版权条例已涵盖电脑生成作品，作者是作出必要安排的人。对你们最直接的是电影节规则，先读。", "section": "Story 4 · Signing", "source": "Reed Smith, 4 Aug 2026; Caixin, 3 Sept 2025; Mayer Brown, 11 March 2026; Lewis Silkin, 4 Nov 2024; CEDB consultation paper, July 2024; Animation Magazine, Jan 2026 (San Diego Comic-Con)."}, {"number": 70, "key": "slide-70", "title": "The art exception: a line in the EU rules written for you", "script": "This is a line I love, because lawmakers actually thought about you. The EU's rule says that if the deepfake is part of an evidently artistic, creative, satirical or fictional work, you must still disclose, but in a way that doesn't hamper the display or enjoyment of the work. So you don't need a big label on screen for the whole film. An opening notice, or accompanying text, or end credits may be enough. The guidance says fully AI actors, synthetic voices, de-ageing and digital resurrection are likely deepfakes. AI backgrounds and effects are likely not. And careful: advertising is unlikely to count as art, even if it's beautiful. So disclosure now has an aesthetic. The question for you as an artist is how to do it well.", "zh": "这一条我很喜欢，立法者想到了你们。如果深度伪造属于明显的艺术、创意、讽刺或虚构作品，仍须披露，但方式不应妨碍作品的展示和欣赏。所以不必全程挂大标签，片头提示、附带文字或片尾字幕可能就够。全AI演员、合成声音、减龄、数字复活可能算深度伪造；AI背景和特效可能不算。广告可能不算艺术。披露现在有了美学：如何做得好。", "section": "Story 4 · Signing", "source": "EU AI Act Art. 50(4); Commission Guidelines, 20 July 2026, as summarised by Reed Smith, 4 Aug 2026. Not legal advice."}, {"number": 71, "key": "slide-71", "title": "The artist’s dilemma: a label costs you, and hiding costs more", "script": "So what do you do? If you label it, the research says people will value it less, even for the same image. If you hide it, you might get found out, by experts or by a watermark, and you'll lose much more than a few points. Remember that one hopeful result from the study: a stronger story reduced the bias. So here's my advice. Don't treat this as a warning label, like on cigarettes. Treat it like film credits. Nobody thinks a film is worse because it lists the cinematographer. Say what you did, proudly, including the forty-one attempts and the six you kept.", "zh": "怎么办？标注，研究说人们会低估它。隐瞒，可能被专家或水印发现，失去的更多。记住研究里的好消息：更强的故事能减少偏见。建议：别把它当香烟警告，当电影字幕。没人因为列出摄影师就觉得电影更差。自豪地说你做了什么，包括41次尝试和留下的6条。", "section": "Story 4 · Signing", "source": ""}, {"number": 72, "key": "slide-72", "title": "Signing also means asking", "script": "A signature is also about what you asked for. Your sources: in February, the film industry's main groups attacked Seedance 2.0 over generated likenesses and characters, and Disney sent a cease-and-desist letter. So don't base your piece on those. Your subjects: you remember the rule in Assignment 2. No real person's face or voice without consent, and nothing misleading. And your own work: you can search for it with Have I Been Trained, you can protect it from style copying with Glaze from the University of Chicago, and you can sign it with a do-not-train request. These aren't locks, but they're a way to say what you want.", "zh": "署名也关乎你问过谁。素材：2月电影业主要团体抨击 Seedance 2.0 生成的肖像和角色，迪士尼发了律师函，别以此为基础。对象：作业2的规则，没有同意不用真人的脸和声音，不做误导内容。你自己的作品：用 Have I Been Trained 查，用芝加哥大学的 Glaze 防风格模仿，签上“请勿训练”。这些不是锁，但是表达意愿的方式。", "section": "Story 4 · Signing", "source": "AP, Feb 2026; AMCC 5160 Assignment 2 brief; haveibeentrained.com (Spawning, Lecture 05); glaze.cs.uchicago.edu."}, {"number": 73, "key": "slide-73", "title": "Useful: a credit block you can copy for A2 and A3", "script": "So here's the template. I recommend it for Assignment 2 and Assignment 3. A3's report already has a credits section, and A2 asks you to credit your source. Title, length, and medium. Director: you. Models and versions, since you learned in Lecture 7 to pin them. Then human work: and please count. Forty-one attempts, six selected, edit and grade. That's your story, and we saw that stories help. References: your own photos, or credited. Consent: say it, even when the answer is, no real person. And one plain sentence of disclosure. Put this in the end credits and in your report. Note how it reads. It doesn't read like an apology. It reads like a film.", "zh": "模板，作业2、3都可用：作业3的报告本来就有署名部分，作业2要求注明来源。标题、时长、媒介；导演：你；模型和版本（第7讲说过要固定版本）；人的工作，请数一数：41次尝试、选6条、剪辑调色，这是你的故事；参考：自己的照片或注明来源；同意：说明，哪怕是“没有真人”；一句平实的披露。放在片尾和报告里。读起来不像道歉，像电影。", "section": "Story 4 · Signing", "source": "Template for this course. Example details are invented for illustration. A3’s report already has a credits section; A2 asks you to credit your source."}, {"number": 74, "key": "slide-74", "title": "Write the credit block for your A2 or A3, as it stands today.", "script": "Three minutes. Open your phone or laptop. Write this block for your own Assignment 2 or 3 as it stands today. You'll probably find a hole: a model version you forgot, a reference you can't trace, a person you didn't ask. That hole is your homework, and much better to find it now than on November 30th. If you finish early, go to contentcredentials.org slash verify, and try it on an image you got from an AI tool.", "zh": "三分钟。为你的作业2或3写这段署名。你多半会发现漏洞：忘了模型版本、找不到参考来源、没问过某个人。这就是你的作业，比11月30日才发现好得多。写完的去 contentcredentials.org/verify 试试你从AI工具得到的图。", "section": "Story 4 · Signing", "source": ""}, {"number": 75, "key": "slide-75", "title": "Four stories, one line each", "script": "Let's collect tonight. Looking: say which question you're answering, and look before you judge. Measuring: scores see a narrow thing, and one of them is already inside your model. Showing: critics argue about what a museum is for, and consent has two layers. Signing: a credential proves things when it's present and nothing when it's absent. So credit your work, and don't apologise for it.", "zh": "总结。看：说清在答哪个问题，先看后判。量：分数看得很窄，而且已经在模型里了。展：评论家在争美术馆的意义，同意有两层。署名：凭证在时能证明，不在时什么也证明不了。去署名，别道歉。", "section": "Debate and wrap-up", "source": ""}, {"number": 76, "key": "slide-76", "title": "Debate: you run the Madison museum. Do you show Diffuse Control?", "script": "Now the debate. You are the director of the Madison museum. Do you show Diffuse Control? The left half of the room is side A: yes, show it. Museums exist to show what art is becoming, and to host this argument. No blanket bans, and explain how it works on the wall. The right half is side B: no, not on this model. A museum's name gives legitimacy, and the artists in the training data were never asked. Commission a model trained on consented data, or wait. You have four minutes to prepare, then two speakers from each side. You've got to argue your side even if you disagree. And please say which of our four questions you're answering.", "zh": "辩论。你是麦迪逊美术馆馆长，展不展 Diffuse Control？左半边：展。美术馆就是展示艺术正在变成什么、容纳争论；不设全面禁令；墙上说明原理。右半边：不展这个模型。美术馆的名字赋予合法性，训练数据里的艺术家没被问过；委托用经同意数据训练的模型，或者等。准备四分钟，每边两人发言。即使你不同意也要为己方辩。说清你在答四个问题中的哪一个。", "section": "Debate and wrap-up", "source": "Case: Wisconsin Public Radio, 25 Sept 2026. Sides are for class debate and do not state my view."}, {"number": 77, "key": "slide-77", "title": "This week", "script": "For this week. Quiz 10 is on the course site, in the usual format. Assignments 2 and 3 are due November 30th. Add your credit block, and please look at your own clip for three minutes before you submit. Final presentations are on November 17th and 24th. We'll use the Critical Response Process. You ask first. Bring your wall text and your one constraint. Next week we talk about video reasoning, creative agents and speculative practice, plus coaching for your presentations.", "zh": "本周：第10次小测在课程网站。作业2、3于11月30日截止，加上署名，交之前看自己的片子三分钟。期末展示11月17、24日，用 Critical Response Process，你先提问，带上墙上标签和你的约束。下周讲视频推理、创意智能体和思辨实践，并辅导展示。", "section": "Debate and wrap-up", "source": ""}, {"number": 78, "key": "slide-78", "title": "Three readings for this week", "script": "Three readings, all short. Eryk Salvaggio's How to Read an AI Image: try his steps on one image of your own. The LAION Aesthetics blog post: it's short, and you can look at the example grids by score, and decide what you think of that taste. And the Wisconsin Public Radio report on the Madison show. For Quiz 10, you reflect on one of these. There are also optional readings, and if you only pick one, read Jennifer Roberts. It's only a few pages, and it may change how you look at everything.", "zh": "三篇短阅读。Salvaggio《如何阅读AI图像》，用他的步骤读一张自己的图。LAION 美学博客，很短，看按分数排列的示例，判断你怎么看这种品味。威斯康星公共广播关于麦迪逊展览的报道。小测反思其中一篇。选读中只挑一篇的话，读 Jennifer Roberts，几页，可能改变你看一切的方式。", "section": "Debate and wrap-up", "source": ""}, {"number": 79, "key": "slide-79", "title": "All links in one place", "script": "Every link from tonight is here, and on the course website.", "zh": "今晚所有链接都在这里，也在课程网站上。", "section": "Debate and wrap-up", "source": ""}, {"number": 80, "key": "slide-80", "title": "Generating is now cheap. Looking, deciding, and signing your name are not.", "script": "Let me end with one sentence. Generating is now cheap. Looking, deciding, and signing your name are not. That is what this course has really been about. If a machine can make the thousand versions, then your job is to be the person who can say which one, and why, and who will put their name on it. Thank you, and see you next week.", "zh": "最后一句：生成现在很便宜，看、决定、署上你的名字并不便宜。这才是这门课真正讲的。机器能做一千个版本，你的工作是说出哪一个、为什么，并署上你的名字。谢谢，下周见。", "section": "Debate and wrap-up", "source": "Demo clips and images belong to their creators, shown for teaching and criticism. Full source list on the lecture page."}, {"number": 81, "key": "slide-81", "title": "Crit sheet: copy this for your final presentation", "script": "Appendix, for your own use. Here's a one-page crit sheet for the final presentations.", "zh": "附录，一页点评表，期末展示用。", "section": "Appendix · Do it yourself", "source": "Adapted from Liz Lerman’s Critical Response Process; Salvaggio’s reading questions."}, {"number": 82, "key": "slide-82", "title": "Toolkit: compare your own versions and sign your work", "script": "Here are the commands, if you want to try these yourself. Metrics are good for comparing versions of your own pipeline. And here's how to read or sign Content Credentials.", "zh": "想自己试的命令。指标适合比较自己流程的版本。这里也有读取和签名内容凭证的方法。", "section": "Appendix · Do it yourself", "source": ""}, {"number": 83, "key": "slide-83", "title": "Review checklist before you show a generated piece", "script": "And a final checklist for anything you show: look at it, check your default, watch it through time, check consent, check labels, and write the wall text.", "zh": "展示前的清单：看它，查默认，按时间看完，查同意，查标注，写墙上标签。", "section": "Appendix · Do it yourself", "source": ""}];
const CHAPTERS=[{"title": "Opening", "first": 1, "last": 6}, {"title": "Story 1 · Looking", "first": 7, "last": 23}, {"title": "Story 2 · Measuring", "first": 24, "last": 45}, {"title": "Story 3 · Showing", "first": 46, "last": 61}, {"title": "Story 4 · Signing", "first": 62, "last": 74}, {"title": "Debate and wrap-up", "first": 75, "last": 80}, {"title": "Appendix · Do it yourself", "first": 81, "last": 83}];
